# MAGIC BLOCK — he puts it down and it becomes a BLOCK that does something to the world around it
#: a lucky block that pops presents when he breaks it, a block that pulls things in, a cloud
# that bounces him, a block that zaps monsters.
#
# WHY NOT `placed`. A placed thing is an invisible marker on a two-second beat, and every placed
# wish already granted must keep working exactly as it does. This shape adds what `placed` cannot
# say: a BODY he can see and break, a WHEN (a beat, something coming near, something standing on it,
# or being broken), a WHO and a reach. Those are settings chosen by make_item's `block` object and
# arrive here as the placeholders below — nothing in this file is named after a thing a child asked
# for, and nothing in it changes per wish except those settings.
#
# The anchor is the one the machine shape uses: vanilla drops a marker tagged `_new` on the cell he
# aimed at, and a tick builds the body around it and swaps the tag for `_built`, so it is built once.
import common

define @NewBlock = @Entity[type=armor_stand, tag=${FEATURE}_new]
end

reset
    # The beat and the rest between goes, counted ON EACH BLOCK so two of them never share one dial.
    /scoreboard objectives add mfam_blk dummy
end

function give()
    /function $namespace:give_one
${KIT}
    /tellraw @s {"text":"${GIVE_MESSAGE}","color":"${HEX}"}
end

# The thing alone. A REFUND hands back this and never the kit: the supplies were
# used, and a machine broken and re-placed must not mint a second set of them each time.
function give_one()
${GIVES}
end

# ── Putting it down ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

function build_new()
    as @NewBlock at @s
        /tag @s remove ${FEATURE}_new
        /tag @s add ${FEATURE}_built
        /scoreboard players set @s mfam_blk 0
        build_body()
    end
end

# The body goes ONLY into replaceable space, and is never a block entity (make_item refuses one), so
# putting it down cannot eat something he built and ten thousand of them cannot freeze a client.
function build_body()
${BUILD_LOOK}
    /playsound minecraft:block.amethyst_block.place block @a[distance=..32] ~ ~ ~ 1 1
    /particle minecraft:end_rod ~ ~0.6 ~ 0.3 0.3 0.3 0.02 12 force
end

# ── Going off ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

function fire()
    /scoreboard players set @s mfam_blk 0
${FIRE}
end

# ── Taking it down ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Breaking the body is how a child takes a thing away, so it is checked every tick — which is also
# what lets a block that goes off WHEN BROKEN go off the moment it is.
function check_broken()
${CHECK_BODY}
    /execute if entity @s[tag=${FEATURE}_broken] run function $namespace:tear_down
end

# EVERY clear checks what is there first, so taking it down never removes a block he placed himself.
function tear_down()
    /execute unless entity @s[tag=${FEATURE}_undo] run function $namespace:on_broken
${CLEAR_LOOK}
    /kill @s
end

# What breaking it does: goes off (a lucky block), or hands the block back (everything else), so a
# mistake costs nothing.
function on_broken()
${ON_BROKEN}
end

# The undo (remove_item): every placed one comes down, NOTHING goes off and nothing is handed back.
function remove_all()
    /tag @e[type=armor_stand,tag=${FEATURE}_built] add ${FEATURE}_undo
    /execute as @e[type=armor_stand,tag=${FEATURE}_built] at @s run function $namespace:tear_down
end

function refund()
    /execute as @p[distance=..24] run function $namespace:give_one
end

# @e with nothing but a tag and a type reaches every loaded dimension, so one line serves the
# Nether and the End as well — a second `execute in` line would make a Nether block go off twice.
clock tick
    build_new()
    /execute as @e[type=armor_stand,tag=${FEATURE}_built] at @s run function $namespace:check_broken
    /scoreboard players add @e[type=armor_stand,tag=${FEATURE}_built,scores={mfam_blk=..100000}] mfam_blk 1
${TRIGGER}
end

${EFFECT_BLOCK}
